  {
   "title": "How the bigger numbers are built",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "h",
     "t": "Eleven to nineteen: labíng-"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "The prefix **labíng-** is added to the numbers **isá** (one) to **siyám** (nine) to make the numbers above ten. It may come from **labis ng**, *excess of*, so it means *ten and more*. The prefix changes its ending to suit the number after it:"
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Prefix",
      "Used with",
      "Examples"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "labíng-",
       "isá, ápat, ánim, waló",
       "labíng-isá (11), labíng-ápat (14), labíng-ánim (16), labíngwaló (18)"
      ],
      [
       "labín-",
       "dalawá, tatló, limá, siyám",
       "labíndalawá (12), labíntatló (13), labínlimá (15), labínsiyám (19)"
      ],
      [
       "labím-",
       "pitó",
       "labímpitó (17)"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Labíng-, labín-, labím-"
    },
    {
     "type": "h",
     "t": "The tens: -pû"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "To make the tens, the word **puó** (*ten*) is added to the numbers one to nine as an ending, written **-pû**. Ten is basically **isáng puó** (one ten). The ligatures **-ng** and **-g** after the number then change to **-m** to sound smooth before **-pu**. So **isang puó** becomes **sampú**."
    },
    {
     "type": "pattern",
     "title": "Isáng puó → sampú",
     "rows": [
      {
       "label": "One ten",
       "chips": [
        {
         "t": "isá",
         "r": "noun"
        },
        {
         "t": "-ng",
         "r": "link"
        },
        {
         "t": "puó",
         "r": "noun"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "label": "Becomes",
       "chips": [
        {
         "t": "sa",
         "r": "noun"
        },
        {
         "t": "-m",
         "r": "link"
        },
        {
         "t": "pú",
         "r": "noun"
        }
       ]
      }
     ],
     "note": "In the same way: tatlumpú (30), limampú (50), pitumpú (70), walumpú (80)."
    },
    {
     "type": "h",
     "t": "Hundreds and thousands"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Numbers above nine are **combinations of words**, so they all have ligatures. Between nine and one hundred they are written as **one word** (siyámnapú, ninety). Above one hundred they are written as **separate words** (siyám na raán, nine hundred)."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Hundred is **daán** or **raán**. The table shows **daán** after the ligature **-ng** (dalawáng daán, tatlóng daán, limáng daán) and **raán** after **na** (ápat na raán, siyám na raán)."
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "rule",
     "title": "At ('t) only once",
     "t": "The joining word **at** (*and*, shortened to **'t**) is used **only between the last two numbers** in a series: **tatlóng líbo sandaá't siyám** (three thousand one hundred nine)."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "English",
      "Tagalog"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "ten",
